0012-rasterize-color-glyphs-at-logical-size.patch

Draw colour emoji under Metal at the size and position Core Text draws them.

N4 (.afk/research/rendering-audit-2026-10-05.md): under the Metal renderer colour emoji
were ~20% smaller than under Core Text and sat left/low in their 2-cell slot. U+1F600 in
a 36px slot at 2x: Core Text ink x2-34 / y1-33, Metal x0-26 / y4-30; total ink on the row
828k vs 565k. Same with the system font and with JetBrains Mono.

ROOT CAUSE. MetalTerminalRenderer hands the rasterizer a font already scaled to DEVICE
size -- scaledFontFor(font:scale:) is CTFontCreateCopyWithAttributes(font, size * scale)
-- and CoreTextGlyphRasterizer.rasterize draws it 1:1 (CoreTextGlyphRasterizer.swift:9-10
and :62-63 upstream). For an outline font that equals drawing the logical size under a
2x CTM, which is what the Core Text renderer does. For Apple Color Emoji it does not:
the font picks its bitmap strike AND its metrics by point size, non-linearly. Measured
for U+1F600 (.AppleColorEmojiUI): bounding rect at 14pt is 17.5 x 17.5pt with origin
(1.006, -3.5); at 28pt it is 28.0 x 28.0 (not 35) with origin (1.498, -3.5) (not
(2.01, -7.0)). Drawn, 28pt at 1x is 27x26 px of ink where 14pt at 2x is 33x33. So the
bitmap is smaller AND its bearing is wrong, which is exactly the size + offset seen.
glyphSlotFit (MetalTerminalRenderer.swift ~:1234) is NOT the cause: it is computed on the
logical font, returns scale 1 for an emoji (its ink fits the slot), and both renderers
apply the same dx.

FIX. Colour glyphs only (kCTFontTraitColorGlyphs): the rasterizer rebuilds the LOGICAL
font (device size / backingScale), measures it, scales the bitmap context's CTM by
backingScale and draws at a logical-point origin -- the Core Text renderer's own recipe.
Outline glyphs keep upstream's device-size path untouched, so every non-colour glyph is
byte-for-byte what it was (check-render-parity.sh's box/block cases stay at 0 px).
The renderer sets rasterizer.backingScale once per frame, beside fontSmoothing, and the
glyph cache key gains backingScale, because a colour glyph's bitmap now depends on it and
not only on the device size (28pt@1x and 14pt@2x must not share an entry).

Severity: VISIBLE, not silent. Gate: app/Scripts/check-render-parity.sh case (d), which
FAILS with this patch reverted (all four emoji slots: bbox 8px off, ink 0.62-0.69x) and
passes with it (bbox <= 1px, ink 0.98-1.00x). Transcripts in the PR.

--- a/Sources/SwiftTerm/Apple/Metal/CoreTextGlyphRasterizer.swift
+++ b/Sources/SwiftTerm/Apple/Metal/CoreTextGlyphRasterizer.swift
@@ -4,10 +4,30 @@
 
 final class CoreTextGlyphRasterizer {
     var fontSmoothing: Bool = true
+    // Device pixels per point for the frame being built (patch 0012). Callers hand
+    // rasterize() a font already scaled to DEVICE size (MetalTerminalRenderer.scaledFontFor),
+    // which is equivalent to drawing the logical size under a scaled CTM for an outline
+    // font, but NOT for a colour bitmap font: Apple Color Emoji picks its strike and its
+    // metrics by POINT size, non-linearly, so 28pt drawn 1:1 is ~20% smaller than 14pt drawn
+    // at 2x (27x26 px of ink vs 33x33 for U+1F600), and its bearing differs too. The Core
+    // Text path draws the logical size under the view's 2x CTM, so Metal emoji came out
+    // smaller and shifted left/down in their slot (N4, .afk/research/rendering-audit-2026-10-05.md).
+    var backingScale: CGFloat = 1
 
     func rasterize(font: CTFont, glyph: CGGlyph) -> GlyphBitmap? {
         var glyphVar = glyph
-        let rect = CTFontGetBoundingRectsForGlyphs(font, .default, &glyphVar, nil, 1)
+        // Colour glyphs only: rasterize the LOGICAL-size font under a scale CTM, exactly as
+        // the Core Text renderer does. Outline glyphs keep the upstream device-size path,
+        // so every non-colour glyph is byte-for-byte what it was before 0012.
+        let ctmScale: CGFloat = {
+            guard backingScale > 0, backingScale != 1 else { return 1 }
+            return CTFontGetSymbolicTraits(font).contains(.traitColorGlyphs) ? backingScale : 1
+        }()
+        let drawFont = ctmScale == 1
+            ? font
+            : CTFontCreateCopyWithAttributes(font, CTFontGetSize(font) / ctmScale, nil, nil)
+        let logicalRect = CTFontGetBoundingRectsForGlyphs(drawFont, .default, &glyphVar, nil, 1)
+        let rect = logicalRect.applying(CGAffineTransform(scaleX: ctmScale, y: ctmScale))
         if rect.width <= 0 || rect.height <= 0 {
             return nil
         }
@@ -59,8 +79,11 @@
             context.setFillColor(CGColor(red: 1, green: 1, blue: 1, alpha: 1))
             context.setStrokeColor(CGColor(red: 1, green: 1, blue: 1, alpha: 1))
 
-            var positions = [CGPoint(x: -minX, y: -minY)]
-            CTFontDrawGlyphs(font, &glyphVar, &positions, 1, context)
+            // With ctmScale == 1 this is upstream's call unchanged. Otherwise the origin is
+            // expressed in logical points, because the CTM multiplies it back to pixels.
+            context.scaleBy(x: ctmScale, y: ctmScale)
+            var positions = [CGPoint(x: -minX / ctmScale, y: -minY / ctmScale)]
+            CTFontDrawGlyphs(drawFont, &glyphVar, &positions, 1, context)
             return true
         }
